The civil engineering and building works to be provided by the Contractor shall comprise the full detailed design of all permanent and temporary works, including detailed assessment of existing site conditions, structures  and  any  required  clearance  tasks,  supplementary  site  investigation  studies,  detailing  and preparation  of  design  and  construction  drawings,  construction  as  built  drawings,  and  completion  and maintenance  of  all  the  various  foundations  (including  transmission  towers),  structures,  plant  related buildings,  support  services  9buildings,  installations  and  services  etc.  required  for  the  installation, commissioning, operation and maintenance of the equipment and plant for the Facility.
In general terms, a common and consistent set of standards shall be used throughout the facility for Civil, structural and Architectural work. If any requirements mentioned in Section 7 of OTS with respect to any codes and standards, doesn't exactly match with the requirements mentioned in the code and standard EPC Contractor wants to follow within the Codes and standards envelops mentioned in OTS, the equivalent or more stringent requirement shall be followed.
The clear Headroom of for the ceilings shall be minimum 3.0m, OEM recommendation, as per relevant applicable code and Operational requirement whichever is stringent. Following scope of work shall be included for Civil/Structural and Architectural works;

- -Site survey;
- -Topographical survey;
- -Bathymetric Survey if required;
- -Investigation of onshore and offshore subsoil conditions and study of geological formation and seismic conditions of the area;
- -Geotechnical and Geophysical Survey work;
- -Hydrology and Flood risks;
- -Meteorological studies;
- -Hydrographic studies;
- -Meteorology Survey;
- -Air recirculation study;
- -Hydraulic calulations - steady state and dynamic;
- -Logistic Survey for O&M purpose
- -Security Risk assessment Study as per local regulations

- -Due diligence of all applicable aspects:
- a. Investigation of site physical conditions
- b. Investigation of shipping & transportation logistics
- c. Site Survey, including property lines, setting of permanent site benchmarks
- d. Soil survey including borings
- e. Hydrological survey/investigation
- f. Determination of seismic requirements
- g. Determination of flood potentiality
- h. Gather, verify and present all input data to be applied to the design of a technically and economically optimal power plant.
- i. The EPC Contactor has to investigate the local conditions with respect to planning and applicable laws.
- j. Technical requirements from involved authorities.
- -Provide Plant data regarding interface points;
- -Collection of meteorological data;
- -Removal of existing temporary facilities, site offices if any;
- -Relocation  of  any  buildings,  cables  and  pipe  crossing  the  site,  including  replacement material;
- -Site  preparation  including  compaction  of  soil,  filling  of  low  areas  with  imported  fill  and grading of the entire area of the site to the required lines levels and slopes, as required;
- -Soil improvement;
- -Provision of temporary lay-down areas, warehouses, workshops, vehicles, equipment etc. all as necessary for the construction phase;
- -Provision of precast yard as necessary;
- -Earthwork cutting, Backfilling, levelling, grading as required;
- -Soil improvement as required;
- -Provision of temporary firefighting and alarm system;
- -Provision of temporary site drainage including storm water and sanitary drainage including for  the  site,  site  facilities,  temporary  lay-down  areas,  warehouses,  workshops,  housing compounds and camp facilities etc., disposal of sewage as necessary;
- -Provision of temporary housing compound and camp facilities;
- -Provision of temporary roads and temporary fencing;
- -Provision of temporary site fencing including gates, as required;
- -Provision of first aid, site safety and security system for the construction phase;
- -Provision for site office for the Client and Client's representatives
- -Provision of temporary offices and facilities on the Plant Site for the Owner, the Engineer
(Owner's Engineer) and Owner's Operator;
- -Provision  of  temporary  offices  and  all  facilities  for  the  EPC  Contractor;  and  its  subContractors staff.
- -Site services as required for the construction and Commissioning of the Plant such as:
- -electricity supply during construction;
- -potable water, demineralized water, etc.,
- -instrument and service air,
- -fuel supply during construction,
- -telecommunication,

- -All the Foundation works;
- -All Civil-Structural and Architectural works including and up to the Electrical, Fuel supply, water supply, access road terminal points.
- -All  Civil-Structural  and  Architectural  works for Renewable Energy generation facilities if any;
- -All Civil-structural works for carbon reduction and abetment as required;
- -Boreholes for monitoring of soil contamination during O&M stage as recommended by ESIA report;
- -Back up Fuel unloading station;
- -Service water Unloading station;
- -Service water underground buffer tank, pumping station and storage tanks;
- -Fire Brigade building (if required)
- -All types of foundations including pile
- -Civil works for Storm water and wastewater drainage system;
- -Roads, Paving and Fencing works (Recommendations of NFPA 850 shall be applied);
- -Erosion Protection works;
- -Landscaping as per ESIA requirement;
- -Restoring  damages  to  the  existing  properties  if  any  caused  due  to  the  EPC  design, Construction and Testing-commissioning activities;
- -Civil works related to Intake and Outfall facilities
- -Modification for the existing facilities as required;
- -Access to the interface facilities for the O&M period;
- -All the Civil works for and up to the Civil, electrical and Mechanical Interfaces;

The  Contractor  shall  be  responsible  for  the  design  of  all  water-tight  (i.e.  water  retaining  and  water excluding) concrete structures.
The Contractor shall adopt the following design philosophy for the design of watertight concrete:
- All  water retaining/water excluding structures and cable tunnels shall be designed and detailed in accordance with BS 8007 (BS 8102, Type B construction) to achieve a performance level as defined in BS8102  for  a  Grade 2  structure.  Additionally,  all  cable  tunnels  that  are  founded  below  a  level determined as High Ground Water Level plus 1.5 metres (HGWL + 1.5 m) shall be tanked. Where it is determined that tanking shall be required, it shall be applied as a minimum to the underside of the base and to the walls up to the HGWL + 1.5 m level or shall totally enclose the tunnel if the top of roof slab level is below HGWL + 1.5 m level.
- Where the design deems that building basements are necessary, whether above or below ground water level,  then  these  shall  be  tanked  to  achieve  a  performance  level  as  defined  in  BS 8102  for  Grade 2 structure. The tanking shall be applied to the basement structure and extend up the walls to ground level;
- To reduce thermal and shrinkage cracking, joints shall be provided at centres generally not exceeding 5.0 m (or in accordance with BS 8007 if more onerous); joint spacing greater than 5m can be adopted with supporting calculation of rebar ratio for crack control as per BS 8007.
- Concrete floors, walls and slabs shall be provided with all necessary contraction, expansion and control joints. These joints shall be provided by discontinuity joints, saw cuts and weakened planes. Joints shall be located so as not to weaken the structural integrity of the building or structure. Joints shall be filled and sealed with suitable materials as appropriate;
- All joints in the concrete, including expansion, contraction, partial contraction and construction joints shall include a proprietary water-stop system;
- Joint details shall comply with the requirements of ACI 504R-90, Guide to Sealing Joints in Concrete Structures.
The design of slabs on grade shall be in accordance with ACI 360R

For piped services, the Service Water and Compressed Air Installations shall be extended into each building as required to provide wash down/maintenance facilities. The site Potable Water distribution system shall be extended into each building as required for domestic hot and cold-water supplies.  The domestic hot and cold-water services shall comply with the requirements of BS 6700:2009 -'Design, installation, testing and maintenance of services supplying water for domestic use within buildings and their curtilages'. Plant areas and emergency washing/shower facilities are also to be supplied with Potable Water.
The heating, ventilation and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems shall be installed to provide the required inside conditions as indicated in the Specification below. Design, installation, testing and commissioning of systems shall comply with the latest applicable Codes and Standards, e.g. ASHRAE, NFPA, SMACNA, ARI.
The Contractor shall be responsible for the complete design, detailed calculations, equipment selection, construction, controls, testing and commissioning of the whole of the Mechanical Building Services, subject to approval. The above systems shall comply with the relevant sections of the OTS. These shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
- Potable water;
- HVAC
- Service water;
- Service (compressed) air;
- General mechanical plant;
- Thermal insulation;
- Fire detection;
- Fire suppression;
- Testing and commissioning;
- Handover, O&M manuals, record drawings.

Design  calculations,  system  diagrams  and  construction  drawings  for  all  services  and  systems  shall  be submitted  for  approval,  before  procurement/construction  is  commenced.  Design  information  shall  be submitted with descriptions and calculations under the following headings.
- Objective;
- Concept of system;
- Method of calculation;
- Design criteria;
- Calculations;
- Appendices.

All equipment for the Mechanical Building Services shall be obtained from member firms of the relevant Trade Association(s).
The Contractor shall submit full details for approval, including samples where necessary, of the following components that he proposes to use:
- Refrigeration plant and accessories;
- Air handling units;
- Fans (all types);
- Air filters;
- Grilles and diffusers;
- Fire/Smoke dampers;
- Air cooling coils;
- Air heating coils;
- Control systems and BMS;
- Water storage tanks;
- Pipes and fittings for all systems.

Fresh air shall be supplied for the following purposes:
- Fresh air for occupied areas, minimum 12.5 litres/second/per person;
- Free cooling during periods of low ambient temperatures;
- Make-up air for exhaust ventilation systems;
- Building pressurization to exclude dust. Minimum fresh air supply shall not be less than 5 per cent of supply air volume.
Fresh air supplies shall be obtained from areas not subject to contamination from fumes. Air intakes shall be located at maximum available height, to reduce dust load.
Fresh air intakes on large systems shall have inertial sand filters with bleed fans and access sections. Small systems with fixed amounts of fresh air shall be provided with sand louvres.
All  supply  systems  shall  have  two-stage  air  filtration,  comprising  washable  primary  panel  filters  and secondary bag filters, efficiency as required for the areas served.

The building shall house at least the following rooms and facilities:
- o 1 No. Workshop Area;
- o Mechanical and maintenance workshop;
- o Electrical maintenance workshop;
- o I&C maintenance workshop;
- o Store room for big tools and spare parts;
- o Store room for small tools and spare parts;
- o Store rooms for chemicals;
- o Domestic storage room;
- o 1 No. General Stores Area;
- o Offices; (5 offices with 4-person occupancy)
- o 1 no. of locker room (20 lockers)
- o Janitor's rooms;
- o HVAC room.
- o Adequate no. of Toilets and Showers; (considering 60% Male and 40% Female occupancy)
- o 1 No. Environmentally Conditioned Store Area;
- o Overhead EOT crane(s) as required for mechanical, electrical equipment and sized for the heaviest equipment + safety margin as per local law and regulation but not less than 10%
- o Adequate entry and exit plan for the Trailers shall be considered from two different ends of the Warehouse.
The final size shall be decided during detail engineering stage subject to Owner's approval.

- o 1 No. Hazardous Equipment and Gases Store Area;
- o 1 No. Hazardous Materials Store Area;
- o 1 No. Hazardous Chemical Storage Area;
- o Washing facilities;
- o Eye Wash Shower;
The final size shall be decided during detail engineering stage subject to Owner's approval .
This facility shall be considered additionally at a separate location and away from Plant main workshop, store buildings and offices.
The facility shall be fenced and shall have access control (key or badge access). The facility shall be cover against sun, rain and dust-storm.
